-- title:
--   Gram–Schmidt coordinates $v_i(j)$, projected inner products, and the revealed σ-algebra $\mathcal G_s$
-- statement:
--   Let $x_1,\dots,x_n\in\mathbb S^{d-1}$ and let $e_1,e_2,\dots$ be the orthonormal vectors obtained from them by the Gram–Schmidt procedure, so that $\mathrm{span}\{e_1,\dots,e_i\}=\mathrm{span}\{x_1,\dots,x_i\}$. The **Gram–Schmidt coordinates** are
--
--   $$
--   v_i(j)=\langle x_i,e_j\rangle ,
--   $$
--
--   and $\pi_s$ is the projection onto $\mathrm{span}\{e_1,\dots,e_s\}$, so $\langle\pi_s(v_i),\pi_s(v_j)\rangle=\sum_{k\le s}v_i(k)v_j(k)$. The σ-algebra $\mathcal G_s$ is generated by the first $s$ coordinates $v_i(j)$, $1\le i\le n$, $1\le j\le s$, of all vectors; $\mathcal G_0$ is trivial.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Coordinates and vertices are both `Fin n`, numbered from $0$: the paper's $v_i(s)$ is coordinate $s-1$. $e_j$ is Mathlib's `gramSchmidtNormed`; the paper assumes $x_1,\dots,x_n$ linearly independent, and every statement using these coordinates has $n\le d$, where dependence has probability $0$. The projected inner product is a function of any coordinate matrix, so the same object evaluates it on revealed data. $\mathcal G_s$ is the pull-back of the Borel σ-algebra under the map to the coordinate matrix with columns $\ge s$ set to $0$. The file also records that the product prior is a finite measure. The sanity file proves $v_i(j)=\delta_{ij}$ for $x_i=e_i$ and $\mathcal G_0=\bot$.
-- source:
--   Hunter–Milojević–Sudakov, Distinguishability Threshold for Random Geometric Graphs, arXiv:2607.22480 (held version, sha256 1a398ef9…), §2.4, p.6 (PDF p.8); §4.4, p.14 (PDF p.16); §5, p.14 (PDF p.16)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RGGDetect_unifSphere

open MeasureTheory

namespace RGGThreshold

/-- The product spherical prior `RGGDetect.prior n d` is a finite measure (a product of finite
measures); conditional laws (`condDistrib`) of the Gram–Schmidt coordinates need this instance. -/
instance prior.instIsFiniteMeasure (n d : ℕ) : IsFiniteMeasure (RGGDetect.prior n d) := by
  unfold RGGDetect.prior; infer_instance

/-- The Gram–Schmidt coordinates `v_i(j) = ⟨x_i, e_j⟩` of a configuration `x_1, …, x_n ∈ S^{d-1}`,
where `e_1, …, e_n` is the orthonormal basis obtained from `x_1, …, x_n` by the Gram–Schmidt
procedure, so that `span{e_1, …, e_i} = span{x_1, …, x_i}` (Hunter–Milojević–Sudakov,
arXiv:2607.22480, held version, sha256 1a398ef9…, §4.4, p.14 (PDF p.16); §2.4, p.6).

**Formalization Note** Vertices and coordinates are both `Fin n`, 0-based: the paper's `v_i(s)`
is `gsCoord x i ⟨s - 1, _⟩`. `e_j` is Mathlib's `gramSchmidtNormed`. The paper assumes the points
linearly independent; when they are dependent, `gramSchmidtNormed` returns `0` for the dependent
directions. Every statement using these coordinates has `n ≤ d`, where dependence is a null event
under the product of uniform laws. -/
noncomputable def gsCoord {n d : ℕ} (x : RGGDetect.Config n d) (i j : Fin n) : ℝ :=
  inner ℝ (x i : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))
    (InnerProductSpace.gramSchmidtNormed ℝ (fun k : Fin n => (x k : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))) j)

/-- The inner product of the projections onto the first `s` coordinates of two rows of a
coordinate matrix: `⟨π_s(v_i), π_s(v_j)⟩ = ∑_{k ≤ s} v_i(k) v_j(k)` (arXiv:2607.22480, held
version, §2.6, p.9 (PDF p.11), §4.4, p.14). `ipUpTo s W i i = ‖π_s(v_i)‖²`.

**Formalization Note** `W i k` is the 0-based coordinate `k` of row `i`; the paper's first `s`
coordinates `1, …, s` are `k < s`. For `W = gsCoord x` this is `⟨π_s(v_i), π_s(v_j)⟩`; the same
function also evaluates it on revealed data that are not of the form `gsCoord x` (Setup (⋆)). -/
noncomputable def ipUpTo {n : ℕ} (s : ℕ) (W : Fin n → Fin n → ℝ) (i j : Fin n) : ℝ :=
  ∑ k : Fin n, if k.val < s then W i k * W j k else 0

/-- The σ-algebra `𝒢_s` generated by the first `s` coordinates `v_i(j)`, `1 ≤ i ≤ n`, `1 ≤ j ≤ s`, of
all vectors (arXiv:2607.22480, held version, §5, p.14 (PDF p.16)). `𝒢_0` is trivial.

**Formalization Note** It is the pull-back of the Borel σ-algebra under the map sending a
configuration to its coordinate matrix with the columns `j ≥ s` (0-based) set to `0`. The
Gram–Schmidt coordinates are Borel functions of the configuration, so `𝒢_s` is a sub-σ-algebra of
the product σ-algebra. -/
@[instance_reducible] noncomputable def revealedSigma (n d s : ℕ) : MeasurableSpace (RGGDetect.Config n d) :=
  MeasurableSpace.comap
    (fun x : RGGDetect.Config n d => fun (i k : Fin n) => if k.val < s then gsCoord x i k else 0)
    inferInstance

end RGGThreshold


